  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA broad mid level ridge will continue to dominate the weather across\nthe eastern CONUS. Surface high pressure system will continue to \nprovide dry weather, mostly clear skies, and warm temperatures.\nThe anomalous heights of the ridge have led to seasonably warm \ndaytime highs in the 60s, which will continue through Sunday. \nThe air mass remains very dry with dewpoints in the 30s, so large\ndiurnal temperature ranges will continue through the weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nThe mid level ridge and surface high pressure system will get \nshunted east as a shortwave trough ejects through the Midwest region\nSunday night. While the forcing will help generate some rain across \nthe region, we won't observe a large influx of moisture ahead of this\ntrough and it's associated cold front. Also, the progressive nature \nof this system will limit rainfall with QPF values a tenth of an \ninch or less at most locations. \n\nA more amplified flow pattern develops with the next chance for \nprecipitation Wednesday. Model solutions in general agreement with \na low pressure system to our south with a digging system approaching\nfrom the northwest. There is observed model spread in exactly how the\ntwo systems phase. Expect pcpn chances to develop Wednesday and\ncontinue into Thursday. There could be a mix with some wet snowflakes \ndepending on how quickly the systems phase.",
